# Code owners (D-22).
#
# The team is horizontal: every developer has write access (org team `devs`), and any
# teammate's approval merges product code. Only the paths below have an owner: CI, repository
# rules, tooling, root configs and the lockfile, so a change that weakens the checks or a
# dependency always gets a deliberate review.
#
# - The last matching pattern wins, so keep broad patterns above narrow ones.
# - An owner must have write access to the repository, or GitHub ignores the line.
# - Required code-owner review is enforced by the ruleset in .github/rulesets/main.json.

# Lead-owned: planning, CI and repository tooling.
/.planning/             @soadap
/.github/               @soadap
/.husky/                @soadap
/.claude/               @soadap
/scripts/               @soadap
/packages/config/       @soadap

# Lead-owned: root configuration.
/package.json           @soadap
/pnpm-workspace.yaml    @soadap
/turbo.json             @soadap
/.nvmrc                 @soadap
/.editorconfig          @soadap
/.gitattributes         @soadap
/.gitignore             @soadap
/.prettierignore        @soadap
/.dockerignore          @soadap
/eslint.config.js       @soadap

# An app's ESLint config can replace a preset's rules (nest() layering), so it gets the same
# owner as packages/config.
/apps/*/eslint.config.js @soadap

# The lockfile is what CI and the images actually install. A lockfile-only edit can change
# a transitive dependency's resolved version or tarball with no catalog or manifest change,
# so it gets the same owner as the catalog.
/pnpm-lock.yaml         @soadap
